course: history
lesson: 85
title: Olympiad: timelines, centuries and which came first ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Grandad Ray in Leeds pegs a washing line across the garden: «Right, our kid, every metre is a thousand years» (our kid means little brother or sister in the North of England). The pyramids hang near the far end, the Romans in the middle, and the last few metres are crowded with trains, planes and phones.
explain: Which-came-first puzzles trick the eye. Cleopatra lived about 2,500 years after the Great Pyramid was built, but only about 2,000 years before the Moon landing, so she is nearer to us than to the pyramid builders.
explain: BC years count down towards year 1, so the bigger BC number is the earlier year: 753 BC comes before 44 BC. And there is no year 0: 1 BC is followed straight by AD 1.
explain: A century is a hundred years, and its name is one more than its hundreds: 1066 is in the 11th century, 1492 in the 15th, 2026 in the 21st. The trap is a round year: 1900 still closes the 19th century, just as 100 is the last number of your first hundred.
explain: Between two AD years, subtract. Between a BC year and an AD year, add the two numbers and take away one, because there is no year 0. Ella in Dundee mutters «och, that's a wee bit sneaky, ken» (ken means you know), but it always works.
fact: The Great Pyramid of Giza was built about 2560 BC, Cleopatra died in 30 BC and people landed on the Moon in 1969, so Cleopatra lived closer in time to the Moon landing than to the building of the Great Pyramid.
fact: Woolly mammoths survived on Wrangel Island in the Arctic until about 4,000 years ago, so a few mammoths were still alive after the Great Pyramid was built.
fact: BC years count backwards towards year 1: Rome's legendary founding in 753 BC came 709 years before Julius Caesar was killed in 44 BC.
fact: There is no year 0: 1 BC is followed by AD 1, so from 10 BC to AD 10 is 19 years, not 20.
fact: A century is 100 years: the 1st century is the years 1 to 100, the 11th century is 1001 to 1100, the 19th century is 1801 to 1900, and the 21st century began on 1 January 2001.
fact: A decade is ten years: «the 1960s» means 1960 to 1969, the decade of Gagarin's flight in 1961 and the Moon landing in 1969.
fact: Dates from the course: the first Olympic Games 776 BC, the end of the western Roman Empire AD 476, the Viking raid on Lindisfarne 793, Volodymyr's baptism of Kyiv 988, Hastings 1066, Magna Carta 1215, the Mongols take Kyiv 1240, Columbus 1492.
fact: More dates: Gutenberg's printing around 1450, Khmelnytsky's uprising 1648, Cook on Australia's east coast 1770, the Liverpool and Manchester Railway 1830, Shevchenko's «Kobzar» 1840, Bell's telephone 1876.
fact: The 20th century: the Wright brothers' flight 1903, the First World War 1914–1918, the Second World War 1939–1945, Sputnik 1957, Chornobyl 1986, the World Wide Web and Ukraine's independence 1991.
fact: To put dates in order, take the BC dates first, biggest number first, then the AD dates, smallest number first.
fact: The First World War touched five calendar years, 1914, 1915, 1916, 1917 and 1918, but it lasted a little over four years, from summer 1914 to November 1918.
fact: A person who turns 100 in 2026 was born in 1926, so they could remember the Second World War but not the First.
fact: At about 25 years per generation, 1,000 years is about 40 generations of parents and children.
exercise: Put in order, earliest first: Cleopatra, the Moon landing, the Great Pyramid. | Great Pyramid, Cleopatra, Moon landing | Cleopatra, Great Pyramid, Moon landing | Great Pyramid, Moon landing, Cleopatra | Cleopatra, Moon landing, Great Pyramid
exercise: The Great Pyramid was built about 2560 BC and Cleopatra died in 30 BC. About how far apart are they? | About 2,500 years | Some 2,600 years | About 250 years | About 30 years
exercise: Which year is the earliest? | 753 BC | 44 BC | AD 44 | AD 753
exercise: How many years passed from 753 BC to 44 BC? | 709 | 797 | 44 | 753
exercise: How many years are there from 10 BC to AD 10? | 19 | 20 | 21 | 0
exercise: In which century is the year 1066? | The 11th century | The 10th century | The 12th century | The 1st century
exercise: In which century is the year 1900? | The 19th century | The 20th century | The 18th century | The 9th century
exercise: When did the 21st century begin? | On 1 January 2001 | On 1 January 2000 | On 1 January 2100 | On 1 January 2010
exercise: Which pair of events both happened in the 1960s? | Gagarin's flight and the Moon landing | Sputnik and the first Moon landing | The Moon landing and the Chornobyl disaster | Gagarin's flight and the World Wide Web
exercise: Put in order, earliest first: Hastings, the baptism of Kyiv, Magna Carta. | Baptism of Kyiv, Hastings, Magna Carta | Hastings, baptism of Kyiv, Magna Carta | Magna Carta, Hastings, baptism of Kyiv | Baptism of Kyiv, Magna Carta, Hastings
exercise: Which of these came first? | The Mongols take Kyiv | Gutenberg's printing press | Columbus's first voyage west | Khmelnytsky's uprising
exercise: Which of these came last? | Bell's telephone | Shevchenko's «Kobzar» | The Liverpool and Manchester Railway | Cook on Australia's east coast
exercise: Were any mammoths still alive when the Great Pyramid was built? | Yes, a few on an island in the Arctic | No, they died out with the dinosaurs | No, they died before people lived | Yes, they pulled the stones
exercise: How many calendar years did the First World War touch? | Five | Four | Three | Six
exercise: Someone turns 100 in 2026. Which war could they remember? | Only the Second World War | Both of the world wars | Only the First World War | Neither world war
exercise: About how many generations of 25 years fit into 1,000 years? | About 40 | About 25 | About 4 | About 400
exercise: How do you put a mixed list of BC and AD dates in order? | BC first, biggest number first, then AD, smallest first | Smallest number first, whether it is BC or AD | AD first, then BC, because AD years are newer | BC first, smallest number first, then AD, smallest first
test: ★★★ Jayden from Texas, all hyped: «Cleopatra's like, SUPER ancient, basically pyramid-building era, no cap.» Fix him. | She lived 2,500 years after the pyramid but 2,000 before us | Right, she watched the Great Pyramid being built as a girl | Right, she lived about 50 years after the pyramid was built | She lived after the Moon landing, so she is not ancient at all
test: ★★★ Nan in Cardiff: «Rome began in 753 BC and Caesar died in 44 BC, so Rome was 709 years younger, lush.» What's her slip? | Rome is 709 years older: bigger BC is earlier | None; a bigger BC number is always the later year | The gap is 797 years, since BC years must be added | The gap is only 44 years, since Caesar's year counts
test: ★★★ Liam in Dublin: «A man born in 5 BC who died in AD 5 lived 10 years, grand.» How long, really? | About 9 years: there is no year 0 | Exactly 10 years; Liam's sum is grand | No years at all, as both numbers are 5 | About 11 years, as you add a year 0
test: ★★★ Mia's teacher writes «the 18th century» and Mia writes «1800 to 1899». What should it be? | 1701 to 1800 | 1800 to 1899 | 1801 to 1900 | 1601 to 1700
test: ★★★ Sipho in Durban: «The big party was 1 January 2000, so that's when the 21st century began, lekker!» True? | Not quite: 2000 ended the 20th century, and the 21st began in 2001 | Yes: every new century starts in a year that ends in 00 | No, the 21st century will only begin in the year 2100 | No, the 21st century began in 1901, a hundred years before
test: ★★★ Ava texts: «my history test's taking literally a thousand years lol». How many generations is a real 1,000 years? | About 40, at 25 years each | Just 1, because she said literally | About 1,000, one for every year | About 4, at 250 years each
test: ★★★ A comic shows a Viking raider at Lindisfarne in 793 reading a printed book. What's the problem? | Printing in Europe came about 650 years later | Nothing, Vikings printed books | Vikings had no writing at all | Nothing, except the book should be in Latin
test: ★★★ Grandad: «Back in my day, 1969, I watched the Moon landing on the telly.» In 2026, how long ago was that? | 57 years | 47 years | 67 years | 69 years
test: ★★★ Kai lines up cards by date: Sputnik, Chornobyl, Gagarin's flight, Ukraine's independence. Which card is third? | Chornobyl | Gagarin's flight | Sputnik's launch | Ukraine's independence
test: ★★★ Freya in Somerset: «The last mammoths died long before the pyramids, proper job, everyone knows.» Right? | No, a few lived on an Arctic island until about 4,000 years ago | Yes, every mammoth died out with the dinosaurs | Yes, the last one died about a million years ago | No, mammoths still live in the far north of Canada
test: ★★★ Ollie: «The war was 1914 to 1918, so five years of fighting, easy.» What's the catch? | It touched five years but lasted just over four | None; it lasted exactly five full years | It touched four years and lasted three | It lasted 18 years, from 1900 to 1918
test: ★★★ Zoe's great-gran turns 100 in 2026. «She remembers the First World War», says Zoe. Likely? | No: she was born in 1926, after it ended | Yes: she would have been about 20 then | Yes: she was born right in the middle of it | No: she was born after the Second one, too
exam: Identify the earliest of the following years. | 776 BC | 44 BC | AD 476 | AD 988
exam: State the century within which the year 1492 falls. | The fifteenth century | The fourteenth century | The sixteenth century | The nineteenth century
exam: Calculate the number of years elapsing between 10 BC and AD 10, there being no year zero. | Nineteen | Twenty | Twenty-one | Ten
exam: Ascertain whether Cleopatra lived nearer in time to the building of the Great Pyramid or to the first Moon landing. | Nearer to the Moon landing | Nearer to the Great Pyramid | Exactly halfway between the two | At the very time of the builders
